+# Mod-API (ACTIVE)
+
+Opened 2026-10-13, a player: the accessibility mod *"needs to be a module like a rust crate type of mod ... unchained from
+versioning ... it should work online"*.
+
+THE ANSWER, IN ONE PARAGRAPH. A mod for this game should not be a patch on the game's code, which changes a dozen times a
+day, and it should not need the game's source. It should be a **module**: a folder that names itself, names the one small
+interface it was written for, and names every event it listens to - as a crate names its edition and its dependencies - and
+the game should offer that interface and keep it. This page is that interface: `src/mods/` on the page, `app/lib/moduleFiles.cjs`
+in the desktop app, the Modules card in Settings > Mods & files, and three modules in `modules/` that the arc ships as its
+first users (a quest waypoint, a quest panel, a guide trail). Not a DFU member: Ledger A row MODS1.
+
+## THE LAYOUT
+
+A module is one folder, `<user data>/Mods/<id>/`:
+
+```
+waypoint/
+  module.json     its manifest (required)
+  module.js       its entry: one ES module, `export default function setup(dfo) { ... }`
+  src/            (its own sources, if it is bundled into module.js - scripts/buildModules.mjs; not installed)
+```
+
+`module.json`:
+
+| field | |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id` | a lowercase slug, `a-z 0-9 -`, up to 64; **the folder's name** |
+| `name` | what the player reads |
+| `version` | the module's own, dotted numbers, 1-4 parts - **never the game's** |
+| `api` | the interface version it was written for: a whole number from 1 |
+| `events` | every event it listens to (at least one) - its whole contact with the game |
+| `entry` | a `.js` file in its own folder, default `module.js` |
+| `author`, `description` | optional words |
+| `sha256` | optional: the entry's SHA-256, checked before it runs (`node scripts/buildModules.mjs --install` writes it) |
+
+Unknown fields are refused: a typo is not a silent default.
+
+## UNCHAINED FROM THE GAME'S VERSION
+
+`MOD_API_VERSION` is one whole number. The interface is **append-only**:
+
+- an event, once shipped, keeps its name and its argument list forever; a later version only adds events, and arguments at an
+  event's end;
+- so a module written for `api: N` runs on every game whose interface is `N` or more, untouched by the game's releases;
+- a module cannot name a game build (`gameVersion` and any other field is refused), cannot reach an event that arrived after
+  the `api` it declares, and is refused with its reason - not run broken - on a game older than it needs.
+
+The game pins every event at its seam: `test/mods1_api.test.js` reads the source for the call, and pins the whole event table
+name by name and argument by argument. A refactor that drops a hook is red in the change that made it.
+
+## THE EVENTS (interface 1)
+
+| event | arguments | announced by |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `questMark` | `questXZ \| null`, `playerXZ \| null`, `heading01` | `ui/enhancedHud.js`, every frame the HUD draws, in every scene: where the game's own compass marks the tracked quest, where the player stands and faces |
+| `quests` | `views` | `scenes/questBridge.js`, after every look the quest lens makes - `{ id, title, main, urgent, clockSeconds, words, latest: { lines }, target }` a quest |
+| `drawFx` | `renderer` | `scenes/hostMagic.js` `drawFx`, every frame the world pass draws its effects, under the renderer's own camera - the door a module draws through (`createParticleEffect`, `updateParticleEffect`, `drawWorldParticleEffects`) |
+
+A seam asks `modBus.has(event)` before it builds an event's arguments, so an event nobody listens to costs a lookup. A module
+that lists `quests` makes the quest look run even with the tracker, the herald and the marks all off (`modLook`), under the
+same baseline rule the faces keep.
+
+## THE HANDLE
+
+`setup(dfo)` is handed its own handle, exactly:
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| `dfo.api` | the interface version this game speaks |
+| `dfo.id` | the module's id |
+| `dfo.on(event, fn)` | listen; answers a function that stops listening. An event the manifest does not list is **refused** with a warning, not delivered |
+| `dfo.store.get(key, fallback)`, `.set(key, value)` | the module's own settings, apart from every other module's (`dfjs-module-<id>` in the game's own storage) |
+| `dfo.say(text)` | a line over the game |
+| `dfo.onKey('F7', fn)` / `'Shift+F7'` | a key, never from a text field |
+
+The handle is frozen and has no door to the net, the disk or another module. A listener that throws is isolated - the frame
+and every other module go on - and a module that fails 25 times is switched off for the session.
+
+## CLIENT-SIDE ONLY
+
+A module is code the player installed, run in the player's own client. It reads what the game already shows and draws on the
+player's own screen. The bus carries nothing to the server and changes no wire, `RELAY_VERSION` included; a session online with
+modules is, to everyone else in it, a session without. (`modApi.js` imports nothing of `net/`, and a test reads it to say so.)
+
+**A module is the player's own code with the page's own reach** - as trusted as anything the player chose to install. What the
+bus promises is the *shape* of a module's contact with the game, not a sandbox: it is why a module declares its events, why the
+host can check an entry's `sha256`, and why the shell reads only inside the Mods folder.
+
+## THE HOST
+
+`src/mods/moduleHost.js` `startModules`, run once at boot **off the entry's static graph** (BOOT2's ceiling) and only where a
+desktop shell offers a Mods folder (`daggerShell.modules`; a tab has none). For each folder, in order:
+
+1. read the manifest (`parseManifest`); refuse unknown fields;
+2. the folder's name must be the manifest's `id`; a second module with one id is refused;
+3. the interface it was written for must be one this game has (`moduleCompatible`);
+4. if the player has it off (`dfjs-modules-off`), it is **never imported** - none of its code runs;
+5. read the entry's source through the shell; check its `sha256` where the manifest names one;
+6. import it from a blob URL, require a default export, call `setup` with its handle.
+
+Whatever goes wrong with one is a line in the report (`running`, `off`, `refused`, `failed`, with the reason) and the rest carry
+on. `app/lib/moduleFiles.cjs` is the shell's whole contribution: it lists the folders named like modules and reads one entry's
+source - a plain slug and a plain `.js` name, nothing past them (no `..`, no separator, size-capped).
+
+## THE PLAYER'S SIDE
+
+Settings > Mods & files > **Modules** (`ui/modulesCard.js`): one row a module with name, version, author, what became of it at
+this start, and Turn on / Turn off. A running module is switched at once (the bus stops calling it); one that was off at the
+start was never imported, so its row says *On at the next start*. A tab, with no shell, says it needs the desktop app.
+
+Install: `node scripts/buildModules.mjs --install <user data folder>` builds the bundled modules and copies them in with each
+entry's `sha256`.
+
+## THE MODULES THAT SHIP
+
+| module | listens to | does |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `waypoint` | `questMark` | a gold diamond in the view over the compass's quest mark, with the distance, an edge arrow when it is to a side (F7) |
+| `quest-tracker` | `quests`, `questMark` | a panel listing every active quest: where, time left, the latest entry (Shift+F7) |
+| `guide-trail` | `questMark`, `drawFx` | gold embers along the way to the quest, or back the way the player came (F4): the embers are a pure function of a path and the clock; pools lie on the floor as translucent stains and sparks rise additively, so the trail reads on snow as well as in a dungeon |
+
+Each is one folder and lives with or without the others; none reads another.
+
+## KNOWN LIMITS
+
+- `drawFx` and `questMark` are announced by the world-hosted scenes (the street, a building, a dungeon entered from the world).
+  The standalone dungeon page (`?shot` probes) does not announce `drawFx`: it is a test host, not one a player boots.
+- A module sees the compass's mark, which is the quest's place (the tracked quest, a place the player's map holds) and, inside a
+  dungeon or building, the Exact Quest-guidance tier's marks; it is never told a place the game would not already show.
+- `guide-trail`'s quest path is a straight line at the player's own height: a module cannot read the game's terrain, so on a
+  hillside it can float above or sink below the ground, and a wall hides the part you cannot see.
+
+## PINNED
+
+`test/mods1_api.test.js`: the manifest (every refusal), the version law, the event table, the bus (isolation, failure limit,
+enabled/disabled, a module's own store, the handle's exact shape, nothing of the net), the host (running, off, refused, failed,
+hash mismatch, wrong folder, duplicate, no shell), the player's switches, the shell's door (a real temp folder: slugs, plain
+names, nothing past them), the seams by source, and the Modules card over a fake pane. Mutants: the bus's listing law, its
+disabled skip, its once-only warning, the version check, the unknown-field refusal, the host's off skip, its hash check, its
+folder check, the shell's name check, the event table's argument order.
