 PROBE (no suite row - it needs `npx wrangler`): `npm run account` (tools/accountProbe.mjs) stands `server-account/` up in a REAL workerd, applies the real migrations to a real D1 through wrangler's ledger, and drives the whole account lifecycle AND (AUDIT-312 F4) a whole cloud-save round trip against workerd's own R2 - 36 checks. IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE SUITE WAS GREEN OVER A WORKER THAT COULD NOT BOOT: a module Worker reads every named export of its entrypoint as an entrypoint, `server-account/src/index.js` exported four constants, and workerd refused to start - while `test/accountworker.test.js` passed BECAUSE it imported those very names. Importability is not deployability. It also measures what the suite can only assume: Ed25519 exists in that runtime (it is not in every WebCrypto and the token design rests on it), PBKDF2 at 210,000 costs 36ms there, and a token the Worker SIGNS verifies against the key the Worker PUBLISHES, with a stranger's key and a bent byte as the negative controls. AUDIT-312 F4 ADDED ACC2's HALF, and the reason is the same one: ACC2 declared a new BINDING (`SAVES`, an R2 bucket) and seven routes, and nothing in this tree asked whether that binding is really bound - a binding that is ABSENT does not crash, it answers `no-storage`, which reaches a player as "Cloud saves are unavailable right now" with a green suite and a green deploy behind it. It drives the guest wall, a blob refused without a card, the card, the bytes back BYTE FOR BYTE as an octet-stream, the shot bounded apart from the save, another account refused a read and a delete, and the player's own delete taking the object as well as the row. AND IT WAS ONLY EVER CORRECT ONCE: `wrangler --local` REUSES its state, so run two registered a username run one had taken and logged in with a password run one's recovery check had changed - six failures about the last run rather than about the service, and a migration check reading "no migrations to apply" as "no migrations". It stands on a fresh `--persist-to` directory now, and two runs back to back are 36/36 twice. AND IT HAS BEEN MUTATED AT LAST (AUDIT-PW, tools/mutants/acctprobe.json, 4, 4 dead) - AUDIT-ACC named this tool as never-mutated beside password.js and it stayed that way for a day. The question a campaign asks of a PROBE is the inverse of the usual one: break the SERVICE, and does the probe go red? All four do, and TWO OF THEM THE SUITE CANNOT SEE AT ALL - a named value export back on the entrypoint (the original bug: workerd refuses to start over a constant while the suite stays green BECAUSE it imports those very names) and the R2 binding renamed in wrangler.toml (the suite hands the Worker its own env and never reads that file). The other two are a hash cost that leaves a Worker's CPU budget, which only a real runtime can time, and a migration that stops applying through wrangler's own ledger, which is the thing the deploy runs. `--local` is workerd, not Cloudflare: no network, no D1 limit, no cold start. Set ACCOUNT_PROBE_PORT if a previous run left an orphan. PROBE (no suite row - it needs a browser): `node tools/quickLootStatsProbe.mjs` draws the quick-loot plaque AND its stat panel through `showWorldPlaque` - the same seam the four hosts call - over the real ENHANCED_CSS in Chromium, at desktop, narrow and phone widths, walking the highlight down a pile of four, and photographs each. It exists because a player asked for the stats "next to the quickloot window" and NEXT TO is a thing about pixels: the suite can say the side was COMPUTED right and never that the panel reads as one surface with the list beside it. CHARGEN-REFLEX's lesson is built in - the markup is the real `paint`'s, not the probe's, because a harness that writes its own markup measures its own artefact. IT PAID FOR ITSELF ON THE FIRST RUN, TWICE, on faults no pin in the suite could have failed on: a Condition reads "Slightly Used (75%)" and the panel is deliberately narrow, so that row WRAPS - and the value was only pushed right by an auto margin, so its second line sat hard against the LABEL and read as a different row; and the survival SENTENCE rows ("Nourishes for 180 minutes") inherited the fix's right alignment and read as a value with no name. The rows were right, the side was right, the markup was right, and the box looked wrong. 12 shots. PROBE (no suite row - it runs 600 frames of the real rig): `node tools/peerBodiesProbe.mjs` drives the REAL PeerBodies over the REAL `createFpArm` rig on the arm fixtures with a counting renderer - 1, 4 and 8 peers walking out from 4 m to 50 m, a pose every sixth frame - and reports poses+uploads and sprite renders a body a frame. It exists because PERF-ON2 measured the online frame and never the peer bodies, which are its one real cost; it read 1.00 poses a body a frame at every count before PEER-CADENCE and 0.74 / 0.52 / 0.43 after, and still 1.00 sprite renders, which is the open item. The fixture rig is four small pieces, so its milliseconds are not a retail body's; the counts are exact. PROBE (no suite row - it needs a browser): `node tools/mailProbe.mjs` mounts the REAL friends panel over the real enhanced sheet in Chromium with a REAL MailBox whose fetch answers the service's shapes. It runs at 1440x900, 480x800 and a 390x700 phone on the touch skin, with the widest content the letter's law allows: a full box of fifty, a sender at the handle's bound with every glyph, a subject at its bound, and a letter of one 200-letter word and forty lines. It reads back the panel on the screen; three tabs and Close in one bar; nothing in the box, the letter or the form spilling or scrolling sideways; Reply and Delete reached at a letter's foot; the letter's field tall enough; a finger's 44px and a phone's 16px fields; a refusal said in the form with the draft kept; and a friend's row of Invite, Letter and Remove beside the widest name. IT FOUND ONE DEFECT: on the phone the three acts squeezed a guest friend's name to one letter a line, and