-| probehygiene.test.js | 9 | T2 - THE PROBES' OWN HYGIENE, and the only file here whose subject is the tools rather than the port. A probe that lies is worse than no probe, and `tools/tradeModeProbe.mjs` had lied since the day it was written: it booted the exterior host with no `class=`, so the CHARGEN WIZARD mounted and took townTalk's overlay slot, and `townTalk.keydown` - first in that host's keydown ladder (exterior.js:"if (e.code === 'Escape') backButtonHeld = true") - swallowed every `page.keyboard.press` it made. Its MOUSE surfaces address the window directly and worked fine, which is exactly what made the failure convincing: staging passed, the haggle offer appeared, the Yes never arrived, and it reported "the commit did not charge" against a game that charged correctly. The sweep that found it found FIFTEEN, among them bankProbe typing five digits into a character-creation screen and tavernProbe and guildServiceProbe with seven presses each; the same trap had already cost X11c an afternoon at the other end of the same seam. Rule: a probe that presses keys at a host which mounts the wizard must carry a headless-chargen skip. The ALLOWLIST for the probes that drive the wizard on purpose is self-checking on both sides - a member that grows a `class=` skip has stopped being a chargen probe, and a member that never reads `__chargenFlow` was only ever parked there to silence the rule; either fails. Two of the fifteen turned out to be that first kind and were reverted (audit17mProbe timed out at its own `waitForFunction` the moment the skip was added, which is how they were told apart). The third pin is `tradeModeProbe`'s second lie: 500,000 gold is 1250 kg at `goldPieceWeightInKg`, against a MaxEncumbrance near 90, so every sale it made correctly took ConfirmTrade's letter-of-credit branch while the probe asserted coins - it walks BOTH arms deliberately now and reads the window's own weighing rather than recomputing it. T3 ADDS THREE MORE, on the same disease one step further along: a probe whose EXIT CODE has nothing to do with whether its subject worked. T2 caught `guildServiceProbe` printing OK and exiting 0 on a run where its character had been turned away at the Mages Guild door and no service ran at all. Rule: every browser probe must judge something, and must not print three or more states AFTER its last judgement - that second clause is what catches the guildService shape, whose three exits were all setup guards with ten unjudged prints behind them. EYEBALL TOOLS are exempt (screenshot.mjs, musicRender.mjs and four others whose output is a PNG or a WAV for a human), and that allowlist is self-checking too: a member must actually write a file. THE SURVEY THAT FOUND THE SIX WAS WRONG THREE TIMES FIRST, always accusing working code by missing an idiom - the local `die()`/`fail()` helpers, then the `check()` harness, then the accumulate-and-exit shape `process.exit(ok ? 0 : 1)` that castProbe and firstHourProbe use between them for 45 judgements. The vocabulary is written down in the file so the next sweep does not re-derive it wrong. ROAD-E E8 added three, one per probe of Port-Ledger.md:836: the retired `shopProbe.mjs` name must stay absent while `tradeModeProbe`/`nativeTradeProbe` carry its subject; `toneProbe.mjs` may not hunt the `tone: Normal` TEXT row the native talk window draws as art, and must read `native` and `topicMode` instead; and `worldWhereIsProbe.mjs`'s boot-box drain must sit BEFORE its walker poll and judge itself - a modal holds the motor, so a probe that polls first polls a frozen world. |
